Fix a SPIR-V back end crash on a nested struct in a mesh shader output.

Upstream issue: https://github.com/microsoft/DirectXShaderCompiler/issues/8475
Applies to: v1.10.2605.37 (c4d8f4f9). Also applies to main as of 2026-08-28.

A mesh shader output struct with a struct member crashes DXC's SPIR-V back
end. The DXIL back end compiles the same shader, so this is Windows-clean and
Linux-only. It stops five of this engine's shaders, all four material mesh
shaders among them, because PrimitiveOutput in RendererTypes.esh holds a
PrimitiveOutputFlags member:

    struct PrimitiveOutputFlags { RendererGlobalFlags m_a : 6; ... };
    struct PrimitiveOutput
    {
        uint4 m_data0 : TEXCOORD4;
        PrimitiveOutputFlags m_primitiveFlags : TEXCOORD6;
    };

DeclResultIdMapper::createStructOutputVar flattens such an output and records
one stage variable per *leaf* field. SpirvEmitter never mirrors that
flattening: it looks up a stage variable for the enclosing member, which was
never recorded, and stores through the null pointer that comes back.
getStageVarInstruction asserts in a debug build and returns null in a release
one, so the shipped compiler segfaults in SpirvBuilder::createAccessChain.

Three things are fixed, and all three are needed to compile the shaders above.

1. assignToMSOutAttribute recurses into a struct member, extracting one
   sub-value per field, the way DeclResultIdMapper::writeBackOutputStream
   already does for geometry shader output streams. This covers whole-struct
   and whole-member assignment, `prims[i] = p` and `prims[i].f = flags`.

   The recursion is chosen on the type alone, deliberately not on the
   semantic. createStageVars maps a decl to a single stage variable only when
   it has a semantic *and* is not a structure type, so a struct member
   carrying a semantic of its own is flattened too, its members inheriting
   that semantic at increasing indices. Testing the semantic here misses
   exactly the case this engine hits.

   A leaf reached through the recursion inherits its semantic and so has none
   of its own, which makes getStageVarSemantic invalid. The three
   semantic-dependent special cases - clip/cull distance, SV_Position Y
   inversion, and the CullPrimitive exception to the boolean cast - are
   therefore guarded on isValid(). An inherited semantic is a user semantic,
   never one of those.

2. The recursion extracts by the lowered field index, not the AST one.
   Bitfields are merged into a single member when a struct is lowered, so
   several AST fields share one SPIR-V member and every later field shifts
   down. Extracting by AST index produces "Index is out of bounds" from the
   validator on any output struct with bitfields, which is what
   PrimitiveOutputFlags is. FieldInfo::fieldIndex carries the lowered index.
   Because extracting a bitfield yields the whole merged member, the field's
   own bits are then taken back out with createBitFieldExtract; a stage
   variable exists per AST field, so each bitfield is written separately.

3. collectArrayStructIndices returned as soon as it recognized the mesh output
   attribute, so for a member chain two levels deep, `prims[i].f.a = x`, it
   returned the *outer* member and pushed the inner field index into the
   access chain. That index addresses a SPIR-V struct that does not exist. It
   now keeps walking up so the caller sees the leaf declaration, and pushes no
   field index for a struct with no SPIR-V counterpart.

   That branch is reachable only for a member chain of depth two or more under
   a vertices/primitives declaration, which is precisely the case that crashes
   today, so it cannot regress a working shader.

Not addressed: a mesh shader output struct using C++ base classes. The
pre-existing loop in tryToAssignToMSOutAttrsOrIndices already skips base
sub-objects, so that case is broken independently of this fix and is left as
it was.

